    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Longitudes are vertical imaginary semi-circles running from the North Pole to the South Pole.",
        "Unlike parallels of latitude, all meridians of longitude are equal in length.",
        "The distance between lines of longitude is maximum at the equator and steadily decreases to zero at the poles.",
        "The Prime Meridian (0°) passes through Greenwich near London and divides Earth into Eastern and Western Hemispheres.",
        "Earth turns 360° in 24 hours, which corresponds to 15° per hour or 1° every 4 minutes."
      ],
      "id": "k0hnlz6"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Indian Standard Time (IST) is derived from the standard meridian of 82.5° E passing through Mirzapur, Uttar Pradesh.",
        "The 180° East and 180° West longitudes lie on the exact same line, forming the basis for the International Date Line.",
        "Places located to the East of Greenwich are ahead of GMT, while places to the West are behind GMT."
      ],
      "id": "1ev8ds5"
